WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.049 --> 00:00:05.680
Most actors toil away in obscurity for years,
waiting for the chance to finally burst into

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.760
the limelight.

00:00:06.760 --> 00:00:11.430
Not every big break, however, is as satisfying
as it seems at the start.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.880
Sometimes a star gets fed up with their role.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:18.029
Then again, sometimes actors just want to
see their characters bite the dust in the

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:19.800
most epic way possible.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:24.199
What more can an actor hope for but a memorable
on-screen death?

00:00:24.199 --> 00:00:26.210
Harrison Ford - Star Wars

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:31.189
Few roles are more associated with Harrison
Ford than the one that started it all: Han

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:32.189
Solo.

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:36.190
Despite the fact that the role launched his
blockbuster career, Ford initially wanted

00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:39.660
George Lucas to kill Solo off during Return
of the Jedi.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.300
He felt that Solo's sacrifice would lend emotional
weight to the story.

00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:50.040
"And I thought the best utility of the character
would be for him to sacrifice himself to a

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:51.780
high ideal."

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.809
But according to Ford, Lucas was reluctant
to put a dent in Han Solo action figure sales,

00:00:56.809 --> 00:00:58.680
so he kept Solo alive.

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:02.850
That let the scoundrel build a family with
Leia within the Star Wars universe.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:07.660
Meanwhile, those of us stuck in the real,
crappier universe, got 40 years of Han Solo

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:08.660
action figures.

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:09.930
"Why did you do it?"

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.370
"The Empire tricked me, Han.

00:01:11.370 --> 00:01:12.520
I had no choice!"

00:01:13.240 --> 00:01:14.240
"Prepare for freezing."

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:15.400
"Long live the Alliance!"

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:17.020
"Goodbye, my friends."

00:01:17.030 --> 00:01:23.240
Of course, Ford got his wish in 2015's The
Force Awakens, going off to that great big

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.540
moisture farm in the sky.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.200
Samuel L. Jackson - Deep Blue Sea

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.320
Deep Blue Sea is one of those movies that
came together in the right place, at the right

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:33.320
time.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.450
If it hadn't hit right before the flood of
giant monster schlock fests tearing up the

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:42.500
Syfy Channel in the early 2000s, director
Renny Harlin's goofy monster romp probably

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:44.440
wouldn't have made it onto the big screen.

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:50.570
Now, it's a guilty pleasure chock full of
sharks and truly poetic dialogue.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:59.030
"You ate my bird."

00:01:59.030 --> 00:02:03.800
When Harlin initially contacted actor Samuel
L. Jackson, he offered Jackson the role that

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:07.740
ended up going to LL Cool J — Sherman "Preacher"
Dudley.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.890
Jackson declined, so Harlin created the character
of Russell Franklin specifically for him.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:16.760
As soon as he read Franklin's death scene,
Jackson was on board.

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:21.190
According to the DVD commentary, the actor
relished the opportunity to die in dramatic,

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:26.281
horror movie fashion, saying: "I've done a
lot of different things in movies...but nothing

00:02:26.281 --> 00:02:29.120
like what happens to me in this one."

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:33.590
His character's death truly is one of the
most memorable in recent cinematic history,

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:38.440
as well as something of a jab at the cinematic
trope of blowhard heroic speeches.

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:40.580
"We're gonna seal off this..."

00:02:47.020 --> 00:02:49.740
Leonard Nimoy - Star Trek II: Wrath of Khan

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:54.260
Few characters are more widely recognized
than Leonard Nimoy's Spock.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:59.060
Starting with the original Star Trek series,
Spock has had a storied sci-fi career, appearing

00:02:59.060 --> 00:03:03.990
on episodes of The Next Generation as well
as eight Star Trek movies, from Star Trek:

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:07.180
The Motion Picture all the way to Star Trek
Into Darkness.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:12.130
However, the half-human, half-Vulcan's run
nearly ended before his cinematic journey

00:03:12.130 --> 00:03:13.400
really started.

00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:18.480
After the first Trek film, Nimoy decided to
set aside the role indefinitely, if not permanently.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.630
To convince Nimoy to sign on for Wrath of
Khan, Paramount producer Harve Bennett and

00:03:22.630 --> 00:03:26.710
screenwriter Jack Sowards offered the actor
a juicy death sequence.

00:03:26.710 --> 00:03:31.460
Ironically, it was Spock's death that actually
kept Nimoy in the Star Trek universe.

00:03:31.460 --> 00:03:36.010
Nimoy enjoyed his last hurrah as Spock so
much, that he decided to come back for further

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:41.370
installments — and even directed Star Trek
III: The Search for Spock and Star Trek IV:

00:03:41.370 --> 00:03:43.060
The Voyage Home.

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:45.319
Isaac Hayes - South Park

00:03:45.319 --> 00:03:47.800
No one can deny the power of Chef.

00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:52.610
For nine seasons, Isaac Hayes' rich baritone
made the lusty school lunch-prepper into one

00:03:52.610 --> 00:03:54.780
of the show's breakout characters.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:00.380
But South Park's unique brand of satire went
a little too far for Hayes' taste in 2005.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.990
The actor and musician, a Scientologist, was
reportedly offended by their critique of the

00:04:04.990 --> 00:04:09.349
controversial religious sect in the episode
"Trapped in the Closet" — which mocked the

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:13.709
religion's biggest names such as Tom Cruise
and John Travolta, as well as the group's

00:04:13.709 --> 00:04:14.709
unusual views.

00:04:14.709 --> 00:04:15.920
"What's going on?"

00:04:15.920 --> 00:04:17.819
"Tom Cruise won't come out of the closet."

00:04:17.819 --> 00:04:18.819
"What?"

00:04:18.819 --> 00:04:19.819
"Just leave me alone!"

00:04:19.819 --> 00:04:23.940
"Well, we can't leave you alone because you
won't come out of the closet!"

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:27.669
The episode was apparently the straw that
broke Hayes' back, because the musician told

00:04:27.669 --> 00:04:32.169
the Stone and Parker he wanted out of his
contract due to South Park's "growing insensitivity

00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:34.270
towards personal spiritual beliefs."

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:38.669
The character met a fittingly over-the-top
end on the show, as Chef was killed after

00:04:38.669 --> 00:04:43.919
being struck by lightning, impaled on a branch,
and attacked by a mountain lion and a bear.

00:04:43.919 --> 00:04:47.639
It was a classically South Park end to a classic
character.

00:04:47.639 --> 00:04:49.050
"Oh my God.

00:04:49.050 --> 00:04:50.180
They killed Chef."

00:04:51.210 --> 00:04:53.400
Dean Norris - Breaking Bad

00:04:53.409 --> 00:04:58.070
Breaking Bad is one of those shows that came
out of nowhere and blew everybody away, thanks

00:04:58.070 --> 00:05:01.800
in no small part to Bryan Cranston's performance
as Walter White.

00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:05.020
"A guy opens his door and gets shot, and you
think that of me?

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:05.520
No.

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.700
I am the one who knocks!"

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.939
Aside from its intriguing premise and tightly
coiled suspense, the show's true heart came

00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:18.289
from a host of offbeat characters that gave
the morally gray dramedy a rabid fanbase.

00:05:18.289 --> 00:05:22.460
Of those, Dean Norris' Hank Schrader rapidly
became a fan favorite.

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:26.860
As the series progressed to its fifth and
final season, though, Norris' thespian cred

00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:29.249
grew and acting gigs abounded.

00:05:29.249 --> 00:05:33.289
In order to potentially start the next phase
of his career, the actor asked the producers

00:05:33.289 --> 00:05:37.530
if they could take out his character halfway
through the last season, to which they replied:

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.189
"we kind of need you for the last eight [episodes].

00:05:40.189 --> 00:05:42.889
We've been building that up for the last five
years."

00:05:42.889 --> 00:05:46.990
In light of the series' shocking conclusion,
Norris was ultimately glad they convinced

00:05:46.990 --> 00:05:48.009
him to stay.

00:05:48.009 --> 00:05:50.500
So are the show's fans.

00:05:50.500 --> 00:05:52.150
Sophie Turner - Game of Thrones

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:57.819
Yes, it's true: Sansa Stark is still alive
and kicking on Game of Thrones, at least as

00:05:57.819 --> 00:05:59.319
of the making of this video.

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:03.419
But Sophie Turner isn't just ready for her
character to die — she's actively seeking

00:06:03.419 --> 00:06:04.419
it out.

00:06:04.419 --> 00:06:09.270
As a member of the powerful Stark clan, Sansa
grew from a misty-eyed tween into a resilient

00:06:09.270 --> 00:06:11.550
young heiress right before our eyes.

00:06:11.550 --> 00:06:16.610
Sophie Turner's already moving in a new direction
as Jean Grey in the X-Men universe, among

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:21.120
other film roles, and the actress has expressed
her desire for the eldest Stark daughter to

00:06:21.120 --> 00:06:23.240
follow in her family's footsteps.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:27.189
In an interview, Turner joked: "If you're
on Game of Thrones and you don't have a cool

00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:29.270
death scene, then what's the point?"

00:06:29.270 --> 00:06:30.669
Ain't that the truth.

00:06:30.669 --> 00:06:35.349
Fortunately for Sansa, the HBO juggernaut
is preparing to take its final bow, so she

00:06:35.349 --> 00:06:37.490
may get her wish after all.

00:06:37.490 --> 00:06:42.740
At the same time, writer George R.R. Martin's
final books have yet to arrive on store shelves,

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:46.860
so we don't have any official word on the
fate of Ms. Stark — assuming the series

00:06:46.860 --> 00:06:50.080
stays remotely close the books from here on
out, anyway.

00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:52.149
When it comes to Game of Thrones, well....

00:06:52.149 --> 00:06:53.969
"You know nothing."

00:06:53.969 --> 00:06:56.149
Sean Bean - Lord of the Rings

00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:59.039
The Fellowship of the Ring reintroduced J.R.R.

00:06:59.039 --> 00:07:03.899
Tolkien's classic fantasy series to the general
public in a major way, and a lot of the films'

00:07:03.899 --> 00:07:07.680
beauty came from the epic filming locations
in the mountains of New Zealand.

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.919
The problem was, Sean Bean, who played Boromir,
doesn't like flying.

00:07:11.919 --> 00:07:16.759
When he realized that Peter Jackson intended
to shoot extensively on remote, rocky locales

00:07:16.759 --> 00:07:20.139
around New Zealand, he decided to get there
the old-fashioned way.

00:07:20.139 --> 00:07:23.439
"And then they'd drop me off there and I'd
sort of hike another half a mile…"

00:07:23.439 --> 00:07:24.439
"Wow!"

00:07:24.439 --> 00:07:26.310
"Just because I didn't want to get on the
helicopter."

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:31.129
Basically, Bean would put on his Boromir costume
and trek up the mountain on foot to reach

00:07:31.129 --> 00:07:32.129
the set.

00:07:32.129 --> 00:07:36.740
As many times as the actor has died on-screen,
he's said that the death of Boromir in Fellowship

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.569
was easily his favorite.

00:07:38.569 --> 00:07:39.569
But come on.

00:07:39.569 --> 00:07:40.879
It's gotta be Goldeneye.

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:41.540
Everyone knows it.

00:07:43.400 --> 00:07:45.040
"Ahhhh!"

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:48.240
Thanks for watching!

00:07:48.249 --> 00:07:50.949
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:07:50.949 --> 00:07:53.669
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

